EXT. SALOON PORCH - DUSK

A false-fronted saloon at the dry end of a dust street. The sun sinks behind a ridge, throwing long shadows. A tin windmill clanks.

SHERIFF JEREMIAH "JERRY" ACHTERBERG-NWOSU, 50s, sits in a rocker with a shotgun across his knees. He rolls a cigarette with slow, thick fingers.

On the step below him, SADIE MAE FAIRWEATHER-ODUYA, 20s, sweeps dust with a broom and does not look up. Her apron is stained.

Leaning on a post, a stranger in a flat-brimmed hat watches the street. AUGUSTUS "GUS" KOWALCZYK-LAW, 40s, drover, spurs jingling when he shifts.

JERRY
Evening, Gus.

GUS
Sheriff.

JERRY
Cattle all watered?

GUS
Every last head.

SADIE MAE
Don't see why you need a watered cow to rob a bank.

Jerry's cigarette stops halfway to his lip. Gus does not move. The windmill clanks.

JERRY
Sadie Mae.

SADIE MAE
I'm sweeping, Sheriff.

JERRY
You're talking.

SADIE MAE
Can't I do both?

From the far end of the street, a horse picks its way through the dusk, hooves soft in the powder. Its rider is a woman in black, face shadowed by a widow's veil. She reins in at the foot of the porch and dismounts with a stiff, practised ease.

MADAME PRUDENCE LOCKHART-ADEYEMI, 60s, black dress, carpetbag, steel in her eye. She lifts her veil, studies the three of them one by one, and drops a letter on the porch boards where Jerry will have to look at it.

PRUDENCE
Sheriff. Your name, I'm told, is on this.

JERRY
Ma'am.

PRUDENCE
My husband owned the bank. He died last Tuesday. Someone in this town knows how.

Jerry lowers the shotgun. Gus pushes off his post. Sadie Mae sets down the broom, and for the first time, looks Prudence in the eye.

JERRY
Mrs. Lockhart-Adeyemi, I'm sorry for your loss.

PRUDENCE
Don't be sorry. Be useful.

GUS
(low)
Ma'am, I'd be careful what you say on this porch.

PRUDENCE
Mr. Kowalczyk-Law. You are the last man in this territory to give me advice on caution.

Gus' hand drifts to his belt. Jerry thumbs the shotgun's hammer, easy. Sadie Mae steps back on the stairs and the broom clatters behind her. Prudence does not flinch, standing in the half-light with her carpetbag held in front of her like a Bible.

PRUDENCE (CONT'D)
(quietly)
Someone has until sunrise.

She steps up onto the porch. Walks between them, through the swing doors, into the saloon. The doors flap and settle.

A stable boy, EZEKIEL "PIPPIN" PAPADOPOULOS, 12, sprints up the street, grabs the widow's reins, and gapes at the three of them.

PIPPIN
Ma'am says to water her horse and not ask.

JERRY
Well.

SADIE MAE
Well.

GUS
Well, hell.

Jerry takes up the shotgun. Gus tips his hat. Sadie Mae gathers her broom. Not one of them looks at another.
